The bug in the original code was that it returned an empty list (`[]`) when the input number `n` was a prime number. This is because when `n` is prime, the loop completes without finding any factors, and the function reaches the `return []` statement at the end.

To fix this, we need to handle the case when `n` is prime. If the loop completes without finding any factors, it means that `n` itself is a prime factor. In that case, we should return a list containing only `n`.

So, the fixed code adds the line `return [n]` at the end of the function, instead of returning an empty list. This ensures that when `n` is prime, the function returns a list with `n` as the only factor.

With this fix, the `get_factors` function will correctly return the list of prime factors for any positive integer `n`.

